===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A grouping based on shared characteristics; a [[class]].
#: {{ux|en|This '''type''' of plane can handle rough weather more easily than that '''type''' of plane.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=2012-03|author=Lee A. Groat|volume=100|issue=2|page=128|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Gemstones|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/2/gemstones|passage=Although there are dozens of different '''types''' of gems, among the best known and most important are diamond, ruby and sapphire, emerald and other gem forms of the mineral beryl, chrysoberyl, tanzanite, tsavorite, topaz and jade.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120614165157/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/2/gemstones|archivedate=14 June 2012}}
## {{lb|en|literature}} classification of [[text]]s according to their [[literary]] [[style]]
# An individual considered [[typical]] of its class, one regarded as typifying a certain profession, environment, etc.
#* {{quote-av|en|writer={{w|Waldo Salt}}|actor={{w|Dustin Hoffman}}|role=Enrico "Ratso" Rizzo|title={{w|Midnight Cowboy}}|publisher={{w|United Artists}}|year=1969|text=Look, with these gals that want to buy it, most of them are older, dignified. ''Social Register'' '''types''', you know what I mean? They can't be trotting down to Times Square to pick out the merchandise. They got to have some kind of, uh, middleman.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=w:Pat Conroy|title=w:The Great Santini| page=4| passage="I just peeked out toward the restaurant and there are a lot of Navy '''types''' in there. I'd hate for you to get in trouble on your last night in Europe."}}
# An individual that represents the ideal for its class; an [[embodiment]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1872|author=Mary Rose Godfrey|title=Loyal|volume=3|page=116
|passage=Altogether he was the '''type''' of low ruffianism — as ill-conditioned a looking brute as ever ginned a hare.}}
# {{senseid|en|printing block letter/character}} {{lb|en|printing|countable}} A [[letter]] or [[character]] used for [[printing]], historically a cast or engraved [[block]].
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} Such types collectively, or a set of type of one [[font]] or [[size]].
## {{lb|en|chiefly|uncountable}} Text printed with such type, or imitating its characteristics.
##: {{ux|en|The headline was set in bold '''type'''.}}
# {{lb|en|taxonomy}} Something, often a specimen, selected as an objective anchor to connect a scientific name to a [[taxon]]; this need not be representative or typical.
#* {{quote-web|en|date=March 20, 2009|site=Why Evolution Is True|author=Greg Mayer| title=Who is the type specimen of Homo sapiens?| url=https://whyevolutionistrue.wordpress.com/2009/03/20/who-is-the-type-specimen-of-homo-sapiens/| passage=...thus Stearn has designated Linnaeus as the '''type''' specimen of ''Homo sapiens''|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200315053125/https://whyevolutionistrue.wordpress.com/2009/03/20/who-is-the-type-specimen-of-homo-sapiens/|archivedate=15 March 2020}}
#* {{quote-web|1=en|date=December 16, 2015|site=Natural History Museum|title=What's in a fly?|url=https://naturalhistorymuseum.blog/2015/12/16/whats-in-a-fly-musca-domestica-the-greatest-traveller-of-them-all-curator-of-diptera/|passage=''Musca domestica'' is the '''type'''-species of ''Musca'', a genus originally created by Linnaeus for a variety of higher Diptera, many of which are now known to be in other families.|accessdate=23 May 2020|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930031404/https://naturalhistorymuseum.blog/2015/12/16/whats-in-a-fly-musca-domestica-the-greatest-traveller-of-them-all-curator-of-diptera/|archivedate=30 September 2020}}
# Preferred sort of person; sort of person that one is attracted to.
#: {{ux|en|We can't get along: he's just not my '''type'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was exactly her '''type'''.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A [[blood group]].
# {{lb|en|corpus linguistics}} A word that occurs in a text or [[corpus]] irrespective of how many times it occurs, as opposed to a [[token]].
#: {{syn|en|type-word}}
# {{lb|en|theology}} An event or person that [[prefigure]]s or [[foreshadow]]s a later event - commonly an [[Old Testament]] event linked to [[Christian]] times.
# {{senseid|en|Q190087}} {{lb|en|computing theory}} A [[tag]] attached to [[variable]]s and [[value]]s used in determining which kinds of value can be used in which situations.
#: {{syn|en|data type<id:Q190087>}}
# {{lb|en|fine arts}} The original object, or class of objects, scene, face, or conception, which becomes the subject of a copy; especially, the design on the face of a medal or a coin.
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} A simple [[compound]], used as a mode or pattern to which other compounds are conveniently regarded as being related, and from which they may be actually or theoretically [[derive]]d.
#: {{ux|en|The fundamental '''types''' used to express the simplest and most essential chemical relations are hydrochloric acid, water, ammonia, and methane.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} A part of the [[partition]] of the object domain of a logical theory (which due to the existence of such partition, would be called a ''typed'' theory). (''Note'': this [[w:Curry–Howard correspondence|corresponds]] to the notion of "data type" in computing theory.)
#* '''2011''', V.N. Grishin (originator), "Types, theory of", in ''Encyclopedia of Mathematics''. URL: https://web.archive.org/web/20191122171633/https://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php?title=Types,_theory_of&oldid=14150
#*: Logics of the second and higher orders may be regarded as '''type'''-theoretic systems.
#: {{ux|en|Categorial grammar is like a combination of context-free grammar and '''types'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|_|except in the above special senses}} A [[symbol]], [[emblem]], or [[example]] of something.

